WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.067
-Susan, welcome.
-Aw.

00:00:02.067 --> 00:00:04.601
Everybody's live.
-Yes.

00:00:04.601 --> 00:00:07.367
We're here. We're here.
-We're still here.

00:00:07.367 --> 00:00:08.901
-Thank you so much
for coming to the show.

00:00:08.901 --> 00:00:11.067
-Thanks for having me.
I missed you guys.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:12.968
You grew a beard,
so that's how long it's been.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:14.701
-Yeah, I've grown up.
I went through puberty

00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:16.667
since I've seen you and I'm --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:18.367
It's a fantastic thing.

00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:19.601
How was your summer?

00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:20.801
-Working, working, working.

00:00:20.801 --> 00:00:22.267
Did a bunch of movies. Yeah.

00:00:22.267 --> 00:00:25.000
-Wait, I heard about one movie
that you're doing that's a DC.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.434
-DC comic, "Blue Beetle."
-"Blue Beetle."

00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:28.434
-And what's fabulous is

00:00:28.434 --> 00:00:31.834
it's the first Latinx hero
that has his own movie.

00:00:31.834 --> 00:00:34.467
And it's really --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:36.701
-That's major.
-Even better --

00:00:36.701 --> 00:00:41.501
all of the Mexican --
because his family is Mexican

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:44.200
and all the actors were Mexican
or Mexican American --

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.868
and it's in Spanish,
so it's subtitled.

00:00:45.868 --> 00:00:48.167
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Congratulations.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.634
That's a big deal.
-I'm, of course, the bad guy.

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.501
I'm the white military
industrial complex,

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:56.567
so I had a fabulous time.
-Wow.

00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:58.434
-Because there's nothing better
than being bad.

00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:00.400
-Yeah. I love that.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:02.200
-Yeah.
-Well, since you've been coming

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.267
here, I mean, I would say
probably "Late Night,"

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.033
you've been a friend of our show
and I appreciate it.

00:01:06.033 --> 00:01:07.501
Every time you're on, it's fun.

00:01:07.501 --> 00:01:09.534
You brought your dogs here
before.

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:12.701
-Yeah, my little creatures
all passed away.

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:14.067
[ Audience aws ]

00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:16.501
Yeah, I can't talk about it
because I still get upset.

00:01:16.501 --> 00:01:20.067
It was about a year ago.
And I was feeling sad,

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:22.601
and one of my sons said --

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:25.767
I said, "I can't get another
dog. I just can't."

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:27.968
You know, and he said,
"Well, then get cats."

00:01:27.968 --> 00:01:30.133
And I'm bi, so...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:32.667
-Wait, wait, wait.
So you're -- you're --

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:34.634
You mean you like dogs and cats.
-I'm fluid.

00:01:34.634 --> 00:01:36.601
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
You like dogs -- Yeah.

00:01:36.601 --> 00:01:38.968
-I'm very fluid
where animals are concerned.

00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:40.834
I've had cats before.
-You have?

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:43.200
-Then I had dogs.
Now I have cats.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.334
[ Cheers ]
So, he found this advertisement.

00:01:47.334 --> 00:01:50.100
You know, I was rescuing them.
-Yeah.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.267
-And he found this cat.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:56.767
And when I saw the picture of
this cat, it just made me laugh.

00:01:56.767 --> 00:01:58.567
And I think you have
a picture of this cat.

00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:00.200
-I do. This is Ida.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.601
-This is Ida.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:03.067
Have you ever seen
a sadder-looking cat?

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:04.934
[ Audience aws, laughs ]

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.100
Doesn't she look like the saints
they used to pray to?

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.534
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:16.200
-Look at the frown.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.367
-They said, you know,
she's very serious,

00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:22.434
which I should have realized was
a red flag, but I didn't.

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:24.033
-A serious cat. They're all
kind of serious, right?

00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:27.801
-A serious cat meaning
not social.

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:30.267
-Yeah. Yeah, of course.
-So, when I got her,

00:02:30.267 --> 00:02:32.400
she was very feral. She's five.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.334
And it took me a long time
to get her.

00:02:34.334 --> 00:02:36.467
And I thought, you know,
she needs a friend.

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:38.200
That's what she needs
is a little friend.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:40.267
-Okay.
-So, then I got another cat,

00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:43.868
who is the sweetest -- I want to
preface this by saying

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:45.267
I have a tiny, tiny apartment.

00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:46.968
Like, really tiny apartment.

00:02:46.968 --> 00:02:48.701
I moved out of my family one.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:51.200
So small I don't even
have doors, right?

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.234
So, I have this other little cat
that was kind of sick.

00:02:55.234 --> 00:02:59.033
And so, very fragile and just as
sweet as can be.

00:02:59.033 --> 00:03:01.033
And so we called her Sweetie.
-Okay.

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:02.234
-Do you have a picture of
Sweetie?

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:03.534
-I have a picture of Sweetie,

00:03:03.534 --> 00:03:06.000
but I have a picture of Sweetie
looking very sweet

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.033
but I feel like -- is this Ida?

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.234
-Well, you have to separate
them.

00:03:09.234 --> 00:03:10.667
-Alright, so let me just show
you this.

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:11.801
-There's --

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:18.501
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.501 --> 00:03:20.534
Ida did not want a friend.

00:03:20.534 --> 00:03:22.434
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:26.868
-Here's Ida staring down like,
"Here's Johnny!"

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:28.167
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:30.767
[ Applause ]

00:03:30.767 --> 00:03:33.901
-He's going to murder Sweetie.
-Yeah.

00:03:33.901 --> 00:03:36.400
And I was really frightened
because Sweetie was really

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.133
fragile because she'd had
this operation when I got her.

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:42.033
She -- Anyway, it's tragic.
-Yeah.

00:03:42.033 --> 00:03:45.167
-So, then I thought,
"Okay, this went on for a year."

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:47.534
At every opportunity she got,
she would beat her up.

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:50.200
So, I had to be very careful
with him, alright?

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.000
So what do you do?

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.667
I didn't want to give away Ida,
who was the bitch in the group.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.000
But I didn't --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.033
So, what do you do?
You get another cat, right?

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:02.133
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:04:02.133 --> 00:04:04.634
So I got a male cat this time.

00:04:04.634 --> 00:04:06.934
I thought, "This is going to
change the dynamic."

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:10.367
And I got Harry,
who was only nine months old.

00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:13.033
So I thought maybe
he's a little more malleable.

00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:15.234
-You have to --
-Because he was younger

00:04:15.234 --> 00:04:17.901
and he hasn't been on the street
as long.

00:04:17.901 --> 00:04:20.734
And Harry is just
the sweetest guy ever.

00:04:20.734 --> 00:04:23.701
But, of course, what happened
was that he plays with Ida

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:27.734
because he jumps all around
because he's a young cat and --

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:30.534
and Sweetie is like, "No, get
away. I don't want to touch --"

00:04:30.534 --> 00:04:34.167
And he lies on his back
and he does all this stuff.

00:04:34.167 --> 00:04:36.901
-You've become a cat lady.
You've become a cat lady.

00:04:36.901 --> 00:04:38.367
-Yes. So, I don't know.

00:04:38.367 --> 00:04:41.267
The question is now do I get a
dog to try to take care of...?

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:44.033
[ Laughter ]
-Please! No, no! It has to stop.

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:47.200
-Well, I don't know. If anyone
else out there has a plan...

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.734
-Yeah. No, no. This is good.

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:50.167
-I have catnip everywhere.

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:52.367
I've got these plug-in things
with pheromones.

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:54.968
It's just nothing is...
[ Laughter ]

00:04:54.968 --> 00:04:57.000
-I want to ask --
-So that's my cat story.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.067
-I want to ask you about this.
-And my son said --

00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:01.868
-Yes?
-Who got the cats for me,

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:03.567
or the first one anyway, said,

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:06.133
"You know, it's very interesting
with men.

00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:10.033
If you're a guy -- When you're
picking a guy, you know,

00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:13.968
a man who likes a dog wants
immediate gratification

00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:15.767
and someone who's
very dependent on them.

00:05:15.767 --> 00:05:18.934
Because dogs understand that
they can't exist without you.

00:05:18.934 --> 00:05:22.634
But cats, on the other hand,
they can deal with someone.

00:05:22.634 --> 00:05:26.634
If a man has cats, then he knows
that you can be independent

00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:30.968
and he's not as, you know, needy
as a dog lover

00:05:30.968 --> 00:05:34.000
that needs that tail wagging as
soon as you come in the house."

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.534
[ Laughter ]
-Who said this to you?

00:05:36.534 --> 00:05:38.400
-Don't you think that's true?
-No.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:40.601 --> 00:05:42.334
-Well, you obviously have dogs.
-Dogs.

00:05:42.334 --> 00:05:44.667
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:45.934
What are you, a psychic?

00:05:45.934 --> 00:05:49.000
You're a psychic now.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.367
-I rest my case.

00:05:52.367 --> 00:05:53.834
-I want to ask you about this
because I think

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:56.734
it's very, very cool
and interesting,

00:05:56.734 --> 00:05:58.067
and I want to know the story
behind it.

00:05:58.067 --> 00:06:00.067
It seems like you always wear
this ja--

00:06:00.067 --> 00:06:02.000
You bring this jacket out.
Every now and then,

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.701
you break it out, and it's
a very cool letterman leather --

00:06:05.701 --> 00:06:09.367
it's like a "Simpsons" jacket,
and the Internet goes nuts

00:06:09.367 --> 00:06:11.801
when you wear it because they
go, "What is this jacket?"

00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:13.901
And even though the --
-No, they go nuts because

00:06:13.901 --> 00:06:16.667
I'm still wearing that jacket
after many years.

00:06:16.667 --> 00:06:18.634
And they're like, "What's wrong
with this person

00:06:18.634 --> 00:06:22.400
that she's still wearing --"
-No, "vogue" had a headline,

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.767
"Has Susan Sarandon been wearing
the same jacket since 1995?"

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:26.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:26.534 --> 00:06:29.000
"Vogue" of all people.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.501
-Faux pas.
-"Faux pas."

00:06:30.501 --> 00:06:31.834
It's a fashion faux pas.

00:06:31.834 --> 00:06:33.167
-Yeah, that's from
"The Simpsons."

00:06:33.167 --> 00:06:35.167
And when you did -- I don't know
if they still do this,

00:06:35.167 --> 00:06:37.634
but I did a few voices for them
and I --

00:06:37.634 --> 00:06:39.701
"The Simpsons" are just
brilliant.

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:41.267
And they gave me this jacket.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:43.200
And then, as they added
more characters,

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.667
they would put patches --
new patches -- on the jacket.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:48.667
Now, I haven't -- I don't know
if they still --

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:50.133
but yes, that's --

00:06:50.133 --> 00:06:52.934
Is there something wrong with
not throwing out your clothes?

00:06:52.934 --> 00:06:55.467
I mean, maybe it's just because
I'm getting old,

00:06:55.467 --> 00:06:57.234
but I want to hold on
to my old things.

00:06:57.234 --> 00:06:58.601
-No, I love that you have this.

00:06:58.601 --> 00:07:01.033
But it looks great on you.
-Thank you.

00:07:01.033 --> 00:07:02.767
-Do you -- do you --
-I'll tell you what.

00:07:02.767 --> 00:07:05.334
When I wear this jacket,
everybody smiles.

00:07:05.334 --> 00:07:11.000
And so, I'm actually changing
the characteristics of New York

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.133
because people that see
this jacket go, "Cool jacket."

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:19.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:19.868 --> 00:07:21.601
I'm spreading smiles.

00:07:21.601 --> 00:07:22.834
-You really are spreading
smiles.

00:07:22.834 --> 00:07:24.434
You're such a friend
of our show.

00:07:24.434 --> 00:07:25.934
I wanted to give you --
-Get off my case.

00:07:25.934 --> 00:07:27.501
-I wanted to give you
a little gift.

00:07:27.501 --> 00:07:28.767
-Oh!

00:07:28.767 --> 00:07:29.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:29.834 --> 00:07:32.434
It says "Susan" on it.

00:07:32.434 --> 00:07:33.868
-Let me see.

00:07:36.501 --> 00:07:38.334
I think we --

00:07:38.334 --> 00:07:41.501
What about patches though?
Do we get, like, the band?

00:07:41.501 --> 00:07:43.601
-I can send you some patches.

00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:46.100
-Can we get, like, all the faces
of the Roots?

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.567
-Yeah, the Roots. Oh, wait.

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:48.834
This is the new --

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:50.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:50.534 --> 00:07:52.501
This is the new -- Yeah!

00:07:52.501 --> 00:07:55.300
That's the new jacket.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:56.534
Come on now.

00:07:56.534 --> 00:07:58.367
That's what I'm talking about.

00:07:58.434 --> 00:08:00.434
That's what I'm talking about.

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:03.167
That is our gift to you.
-I feel super cool.

00:08:03.167 --> 00:08:06.868
This is the best goody bag
so far of all the talk shows.

00:08:06.868 --> 00:08:08.734
-You're already recognizable.

00:08:08.734 --> 00:08:11.067
You're Susan Sarandon, but
a little "Susan" on there,

00:08:11.067 --> 00:08:12.534
it kind of makes people smile.

00:08:12.534 --> 00:08:14.901
-Then they don't confuse me
with Sigourney Weaver.

00:08:14.901 --> 00:08:16.334
[ Laughter ]

00:08:16.334 --> 00:08:18.701
[ Cymbal crash ]
[ Sad tuba ]

00:08:18.701 --> 00:08:22.000
-That's what I'm talking about.
That's what I'm talking about.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:24.100
-And also, you know,
if I lose my mind,

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:26.701
I'll go, "Oh, right, I'm Susan."
-"I'm Susan." Yeah, of course.

00:08:26.701 --> 00:08:29.300
Let's talk about "Monarch."
This is a big deal.

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.033
-Let's talk about "Monarch."
-It's got a lot of good buzz
here.

00:08:31.033 --> 00:08:34.267
This Sunday, one of the most
anticipated new shows this fall.

00:08:34.267 --> 00:08:36.100
Can you set up what
the show is about?

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.934
-Country music dynasty.
I am the head of the dynasty.

00:08:38.934 --> 00:08:41.234
"I will do anything
for my family

00:08:41.234 --> 00:08:43.234
to keep my dynasty going."
-Yes.

00:08:43.234 --> 00:08:46.534
-And I have two daughters who,
you know, that could be trouble.

00:08:46.534 --> 00:08:50.100
And a son. Trace Adkins.
-Trace Adkins.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.067
-Who is a, you know, certified
major country star.

00:08:54.067 --> 00:08:56.133
-Oh, my gosh.
He's, like, 8'9" by the way.

00:08:56.133 --> 00:08:59.000
-And talks like --
and very Johnny Cash.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:01.934
-[ Deep voice ] Very deep voice.
-And so it's, you know, a guilty

00:09:01.934 --> 00:09:05.701
pleasure of lots of intrigue
and secrets and --

00:09:05.701 --> 00:09:07.534
-[ Normal voice ] Who's taking
over the family biz

00:09:07.534 --> 00:09:10.067
and country music and --
-And paternity and lies.

00:09:10.067 --> 00:09:12.033
And yeah. Do I? Yeah.

00:09:12.033 --> 00:09:13.534
-That's what I'm talking about.
-And who else?

00:09:13.534 --> 00:09:15.767
-I love that.
-Shotguns going off.

00:09:15.767 --> 00:09:18.434
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I want to show everyone a clip.

00:09:18.434 --> 00:09:21.567
Here's Susan Sarandon in
"Monarch." Take a look at this.

00:09:21.567 --> 00:09:23.267
-It's time to show you what

00:09:23.267 --> 00:09:25.567
you're wearing tomorrow
at the musical.

00:09:30.434 --> 00:09:32.000
Ta-da!

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.234
-[ Gasps ] Oh!

00:09:35.234 --> 00:09:37.033
It's perfect.

00:09:38.234 --> 00:09:40.801
-You're perfect.

00:09:40.801 --> 00:09:44.300
You know, Nicky, I never wanted
just to be a star.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.968
I wanted to create a dynasty,
and I did.

00:09:47.968 --> 00:09:51.434
We always knew that with your
God-given beauty and talent

00:09:51.434 --> 00:09:56.100
that it was gonna be your
destiny to continue our legacy.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.367
-Ooh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:58.367 --> 00:09:59.968
Susan Sarandon!

00:09:59.968 --> 00:10:02.167
Check out the season premiere of
"Monarch"

00:10:02.167 --> 00:10:04.968
this Sunday after NFL on Fox.

